-This halves the sample buffer while keeping range metadata, normalization and rendering in float. In a matched Uno R3 build with 128 samples, this saved 256 bytes of static RAM and added 596 bytes of flash. Keep integer sample buffers as integers if they already use less space.
 
-### Select Float16 throughout the pipeline
-
-To also narrow value/range objects, window parameters and retained spline coefficients, select the same scalar for sources and plotters:
-
-```cpp
-using Real = grafici::Float16; // Change this alias to float to switch back.
-Real values[] = {-2.0f, 0.5f, 1.5f, 3.0f};
-DataArrayXY<Real, Real> data(values, 4, {-4, 4});
-GraficiT<Real> plot(display);
+void loop() {}
 ```
 
-Drawing calls stay the same. Explicit windows and options become `WindowT<Real>` and `PlotOptionsT<Real>`; use `full_screen_for<Real>()` where you previously passed `full_screen`. Existing template classes accept the scalar as their final argument, for example `DataSpline<32, true, Real>`. Custom sources use `DataSourceNormT<Real>`.
+`DataArrayXY` uses sample indices for x and the supplied array for y. Here, y maps the fixed raw range `[0,30]` to the plot height; omitting that range discovers bounds from the data. Arrays are borrowed without copying, so keep them alive while their sources are used. Values outside an explicit range are clipped; missing samples leave gaps.
 
-Narrowing every object does not always give the smallest program. Keep float for closely spaced values on a large offset, sensitive interpolation, or values outside FP16's finite range. Spline solver scratch arrays remain float. See the [precision guide](PRECISION.md) for limits, measured sizes and the full type mapping, or the [complete FP16 sketch](examples/fp16_line/fp16_line.ino).
